\ Interrupts =================================================================
\ GBA BIOS handles the hardware-level interrupts in IRQ mode.
\ That uses the SP_irq shadow register, which uses the top 256B of IWRAM as the
\ IRQ stack and miscellany.
\ It does a fairly modest ISR:
\ - Save r0 to r3, r12, r14    (r12=Forth ip)
\ - Jump to user handler in ARM mode: its address must be in $03007FFC
\ - LR_irq is the proper return address

\ So it's a pretty standard ARM function call: r0-r3 and r12 are caller-saved.
\ NOTE: Additional register saving is definitely needed to write ISRs in Forth.
\ But I'm not sure that's worth doing - better to simply set flags for the main
\ game loop to observe, I think.

host definitions
$03007ffc CONSTANT 'gba-isr

label gba-isr
  lr bx,        \ Just return, a no-op.
